5:[["$","$L10","3.3",{"topic":{"id":"3.3","title":"OSINT Tools and Techniques","minutes":14,"summary":"Choose tools for a question, validate their output, and keep collection safe, reproducible, and proportionate.","sections":[{"title":"Search, capture, and public records","body":["Search operators can narrow a public query by site, phrase, or filetype, but search-engine indexes are incomplete and time-dependent. Official registries and court or government publications provide identifiable provenance. Web archives can reveal earlier content, yet a missing archive is not proof that a page never existed. Record the query, date, and relevant result rather than only saving a cropped image.","A capture workflow should preserve the original response or export where feasible, record the capture environment, and hash significant files. Browser screenshots provide visual context but may omit dynamic content or headers. Tools change their interfaces and access rules; document the version and settings actually used."]},{"title":"Infrastructure and media techniques","body":["WHOIS/RDAP, passive DNS, certificate transparency, and public reputation services can help connect domains and infrastructure. Each has limitations: privacy redaction, shared hosting, historical reuse, and delayed data. A certificate containing a domain name does not prove the owner’s identity or intent. Treat infrastructure relationships as leads with dates.","Reverse-image search can find earlier appearances of a photograph. Metadata, landmarks, and shadows may provide context, but stripped metadata is common and location clues can be staged. Do not geolocate a private residence unless it is relevant, authorized, and necessary. This course uses fictional records rather than asking learners to investigate real people."]},{"title":"Tool safety and verification","body":["Browser automation, graph tools, and collection frameworks can accelerate repetitive work but can also leak credentials, execute untrusted content, or violate collection limits. Use an isolated environment, least-privilege accounts, and approved data handling. Never paste sensitive evidence into a random online “OSINT checker” without considering its retention and onward use.","Validate important findings through a second method or the original source. Record tool errors, rate limits, and unavailable fields. A result labelled “verified” by a platform may mean identity verification under that platform’s process, not proof of the investigator’s allegation. Tool names are less important than a defensible, repeatable method."]}],"practice":{"title":"Design a three-source check","scenario":"A public profile uses a company logo and links to a domain in the fictional dataset.","steps":["Capture the profile and the linked page with timestamps.","Compare the domain’s public registration information without assuming redacted fields.","Check whether an independent official source confirms the claimed organization.","Record one tool limitation and one privacy safeguard."],"debrief":"Three copies of the same logo are weaker than one independent, relevant record. Tool output must be interpreted, not merely accumulated."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","dpdp"],"sources":["privacy","nist-guide","mitre"],"labId":"osint","unit":3,"objectives":["Explain search, capture, and public records and apply it to an investigation.","Explain infrastructure and media techniques and apply it to an investigation.","Explain tool safety and verification and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-3.3","prompt":"What is a limitation of passive DNS?","options":["It reveals every keystroke on a server.","It proves who committed an offence.","It can show historical associations but may be incomplete or shared infrastructure.","It is the same as a lawful interception order."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Passive DNS is contextual infrastructure intelligence, not a complete or conclusive attribution record.","unit":3}}}]]
